    "entrance": "Lower observation bank.",
    "exit": "Lower eastern return exit, reached beyond the beam emitter.",
    "solution": "First slide the stone east so its shade covers the central ascent. Uppercut the guard through the higher thin ledge; it lands there and can be approached deliberately. Take its last hit as a pogo, then dash into the high gallery. From above, push the shade stone down: the restored beam lights the protected tally and opens the lower exit. Recover onto the eastern thin ledge and drop through beyond the emitter to the open exit.",
    "nearestExisting": "Run 1 B26 supplied the uppercut/pogo idea but demanded a near-immediate interception. B12 uses replacement beam shade; B29 sends a shield toward a beam. This revision captures the target on a stable shelf, then makes the same sliding shield change from necessary protection to an obstruction of the remote exit signal.",
    "novelty": "The uppercut leaves a lasting, visible target position instead of requiring an immediate moving-body interception. The room then reverses its safety requirement: first move the shade across the ascent, then remove it from above to power the distant exit. The finite rebound and the two uses of the same stone belong to one route.",
